    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c81f7d39_27fe6d34",
        "filename": "ssh/kex.go",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 597,
      "author": {
        "id": 11715
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-02T17:41:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, if it\u0027s negotiated we would have to validate it, which has caused endless issues.\n\nCan we not whitelist a very popular value? (I don\u0027t actually know the protocol well.)",
      "parentUuid": "3eec89de_686a04aa",
      "revId": "1e7b6908c39e0c2b537de14c038bcf029a1f4575",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "01ca9eb5_fc77fc5b",
        "filename": "ssh/kex.go",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 597,
      "author": {
        "id": 5893
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-02T17:58:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is just bog standard DH, but the server proposes the prime modulus of the group, rather than having a pre-agreed modulus.\n\nSince the security conscious user is supposed to generate these moduli by themselves, we can\u0027t whitelist values (Although I suspect 99% of the servers just use the OpenSSH precooked moduli file).  (You can run\n\n time ssh-keygen -G moduli-2048.candidates -b 2048\n\nto see what is going on)\n\nthe RFC says\n\n  \"A prime p is safe if p \u003d 2q + 1 and q is\n   prime. \"\n\nIIUC, any choice for G !\u003d 1 is safe as long as P safe, so we don\u0027t have to check G, just that P is prime and (P-1)/2 is prime.",
      "parentUuid": "c81f7d39_27fe6d34",
      "revId": "1e7b6908c39e0c2b537de14c038bcf029a1f4575",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "35249a1d_2db70fc2",
        "filename": "ssh/kex.go",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 597,
      "author": {
        "id": 16630
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-02T18:13:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My fear is, that whitelisting some \"very popular values\" would limit the application of this key exchange to such an extend, that it becomes use less. I could even imagine scenarios, where the connection could be established in 1 of n attempts, because the server uses a set of moduli, which shares some but not all values with the whitelist.\n\nWhat about big.ProbablyPrime (https://golang.org/pkg/math/big/#Int.ProbablyPrime)? This is used in crypto/dsa to \"verify\" if a p is actually a prime.",
      "parentUuid": "c81f7d39_27fe6d34",
      "revId": "1e7b6908c39e0c2b537de14c038bcf029a1f4575",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d4a30442_f77990aa",
        "filename": "ssh/kex.go",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 597,
      "author": {
        "id": 5893
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-02T18:30:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "yes, you need to use ProbablyPrime() on P and (P-1)/2.",
      "parentUuid": "35249a1d_2db70fc2",
      "revId": "1e7b6908c39e0c2b537de14c038bcf029a1f4575",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": true
    },
